        "summary":  "The article examines how enterprise AI is evolving from isolated agents to interconnected systems requiring sophisticated orchestration. According to an OutSystems and KPMG survey, 71% of respondents expect applications to coordinate work across systems through multiple agents, while organizations face significant challenges around security, governance, and control as these ecosystems become more complex.",
        "execSummary":  "The next major enterprise AI challenge is not building individual agents but governing networks of agents that autonomously coordinate across systems, data sources, and business functions — a shift 71% of surveyed organizations say is already underway. For senior leaders, this means orchestration infrastructure — the layer that routes, monitors, and controls what agents do and when — is becoming as strategically important as the underlying AI models themselves. Customer-support operations running multiple specialized agents for triage, resolution, escalation, and follow-up will need orchestration platforms that can enforce policy, log decisions, and intervene when agents exceed scope. The key caveat: this article is sponsored content (OutSystems/KPMG), so the urgency framing and survey data should be read with vendor positioning in mind.",
